<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Communal Award</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as MacDonald Award</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Communal Award was announced by the British Prime Minister Ramsay MacDonald on 16 August 1932, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/round-table-conferences-india">Round Table Conferences</a>, and extended separate electorates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> to the Depressed Classes, now known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a>, alongside Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, Indian Christians, Anglo-Indians and Europeans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> supported separate electorates for the Scheduled Castes, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, who feared that the award would break up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> society, declared a fast unto death from Yerwada Jail in September 1932. After long negotiations Gandhi and Ambedkar agreed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a> on a single Hindu electorate with seats reserved for the Scheduled Castes.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Separate electorates had first been introduced for Muslims by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-councils-act-1909">Indian Councils Act 1909</a> and were extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, Indian Christians, Anglo-Indians and Europeans by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1919">Government of India Act 1919</a>. Ramsay MacDonald, who considered himself a friend of the Indians and wished to resolve the country's problems, announced the award after the failure of the second of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/round-table-conferences-india">Round Table Conferences</a>, and it drew severe criticism from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The award kept separate electorates for these communities, added the Depressed Classes to them and applied the principle of weightage. Its ninth and last paragraph applied directly to the Depressed Classes, and Sir Samuel Hoare asked for it to be clarified. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> it reserved 19% of seats for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, against 51% for the Muslims and 30% for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, and the Akali Dal, the representative body of the Sikhs, was highly critical of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> saw the award as aimed at dividing Indian society, while many of the minorities supported it. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> was ready to grant separate electorates to Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> but not to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a>, fearing division within both the Congress and Hindu society. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a> that ended the dispute satisfied the other communities, whose electorates remained separate, but rejected any further advance for the Untouchables.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> also agreed to the revival of Swaraj, which became policy when the All-India Congress Committee ratified it in May 1934. The government reluctantly lifted its ban on the Congress and received in return the anxious support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">All-India Muslim League</a>. In the parliamentary debates on the Government of India Act, the Conservative MP Albert Goodman argued that the poverty of the Untouchables should be eased by greater representation in the provincial assemblies, while the Muslim League remained ambivalent about the award.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Communal Award” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>B. R. Ambedkar, born into a caste classified as untouchable, became a leader of human-rights campaigns; at his request the <b>Communal Award</b> of 1932 gave the depressed classes separate electorates, which he gave up in the Poona Pact after Gandhi’s hunger strike.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
